{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe approved Pydantic output, bounded-operation, compatibility, and candidate-tracing children establish the replacement behavior [rebar:serge-monotonous-aruanas] [rebar:amoebaean-hemihedral-vervet]. S2 already converted `_pai_structured` to ONE bounded Pydantic `Agent` run per output mode (native / prompted); this final change makes that one-operation path AUTHORITATIVE for EVERY `mode=\"structured\"` consumer (library, CLI, MCP, and every in-tree caller), proves provider-cache behavior, then removes the obsolete manual retry machinery and any temporary compatibility delegation — WITHOUT breaking patch/import seams and WITHOUT regressing the `structured_retry_limit=0` single-shot fail-safe.\n\n## Approach\nSwitch every structured `PydanticAIRunner` consumer to the one-operation path through the existing facade. Preserve `ProviderSession`, candidate order, output-mode policy, tool surfaces, timeout/transport limits, provenance, and serialized verdict shapes. `structured_retry_limit` is RETAINED as the output-retry-allowance cap. The single allowance N is `output_retry_allowance(req)` = `min(structured.OUTPUT_RETRIES, max(0, structured_retry_limit))` (structured_run.py:147), and it seeds BOTH budgets from that one value: (a) the Agent `retries={\"output\":N}` (via `apply_structured_seams`, structured_run.py:183), and (b) the CONSTRUCTED request budget — `build_usage_limits` sets `UsageLimits(request_limit=req_limit + allowance)` where `allowance = output_retry_allowance(req)` (structured_run.py:429-431). Seeding both from N is what guarantees the shared request budget can NEVER trip before the shared output-retry counter. NOTE the RETURNED bare `req_limit` deliberately EXCLUDES the addend (telemetry and `completion_banking.iteration_limit_for`'s `2*B` inverse read it), so the addend lives ONLY inside the constructed `UsageLimits` — reading the returned/telemetry `req_limit` (or runner.py's `request_limit=req_limit` log field) is not the model-turn ceiling. So `structured_retry_limit=0` clamps N to 0 → zero output-repair retries (single-shot) AND zero request addend — the abstain/fail-safe that overlap/judge.py:212 and the contracts.py:223 batch depend on. Both output branches — `_run_native_output` and `_run_prompted_output` — are ONE `agent.run_sync` with pydantic-ai's IN-Agent `retries={\"output\":N}`; the prompted path's tolerant-parse (`json-repair`) + validator failure is fed back to the SAME model via `ModelRetry` INSIDE that single run (NOT a bespoke Python attempt loop), so a bounded retry adds a model request without a second `run_sync`. Run the complete ticket/test oracle and authorized direct Anthropic plus Bedrock cache canaries before deletion. Once parity and approved deltas pass, remove ONLY the obsolete structured-output attempt scheduler remnants and temporary compatibility delegation; retain deterministic parsing (`structured.py`), the stable runner facade, and existing public/re-export paths. Document the retry layers, shared request budget, complete-or-omit history rule, trace-not-usage candidate semantics, and the reviewed raise-the-limit follow-up policy.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py`, `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py`, `src/rebar/llm/agent_call.py` — authoritative call-site cutover and obsolete-scheduler removal.\n- `src/rebar/llm/structured.py` — retain pure repair and stop/refusal policy; remove only dead retry integration.\n- ALL in-tree `mode=\"structured\"` consumers (exhaustive census — see AC-1 and the pinned comment): `plan_review/{__init__,passes,xcheck,prerequisites,completion_subcall,fidelity_spot_eval}.py`, `code_review/workflow_ops.py`, `overlap/judge.py`, `enrich.py`, `epic_bug_screen.py`, `evals/eval_solver.py`, `workflow/completion_recovery.py`, `workflow/runs.py` (dynamic single_turn override), and the repo-script consumer `scripts/jira_dc_capability_map.py` — compatibility verification and any narrow result projection updates.\n- `docs/llm-framework.md`, `docs/reuse-surface.md` — documented runtime and library contracts.\n- The RP-01 focused runner, structured-output, fallback, usage, failure, cache, plan-review, code-review, and completion suites.\n\n## Testing\n- Run the recovered RP-01 behavior matrix and all focused historical ticket regressions.\n- Consumer census (robust, NOT literal-grep): a re-runnable AST scan over `src/` and `scripts/` resolves each `RunRequest(...)` construction's EFFECTIVE `mode` — constant-folding computed values like `fidelity_spot_eval.py`'s `mode = \"structured\" if ... else \"findings\"` and matching multi-line constructions a single-line grep misses — enumerates every structured consumer and asserts none submits structured work except through the `get_runner(...).run(...)` facade; paired with a runtime contract test that the runner's structured dispatch is single-sourced (one implementation, no bespoke scheduler).\n- `structured_retry_limit=0` fail-safe: a test asserts the knob still yields zero output-repair retries (single Agent output attempt), preserving overlap/judge.py:212 and the contracts.py:223 batch abstain.\n- AC-3 invariant: a test asserts exactly ONE outer Agent run on a well-formed first response, and exactly TWO (native then prompted) only on the bug-895c downgrade — never a third.\n- Run lean/full import, lint, typecheck, complexity, and module-size gates.\n- Run direct Anthropic and Bedrock same-run retry canaries using environment-provided credentials without printing or persisting secrets; record cache read/write/input counts and complete-versus-omitted wire observations on the ticket.\n- Confirm rollback by temporarily selecting the pre-removal compatibility commit against the same fixtures; no stored data migration is involved.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The one-operation Pydantic path is the SOLE structured-output implementation reachable through the `PydanticAIRunner` facade, so EVERY `mode=\"structured\"` consumer — library, CLI, MCP, and each in-tree caller in the exhaustive census (`plan_review/{__init__,passes,xcheck,prerequisites,completion_subcall,fidelity_spot_eval}`, `code_review/workflow_ops`, `overlap/judge`, `enrich`, `epic_bug_screen`, `evals/eval_solver`, `workflow/completion_recovery`, `workflow/runs`, `scripts/jira_dc_capability_map`) — necessarily uses it; verified by consumer contract tests, by a mode-resolving AST census (not a literal grep) that fails if any structured `RunRequest` is submitted outside `get_runner(...).run(...)`, and by a runner contract test that structured dispatch is single-sourced.\n- [ ] The recovered RP-01 behavior matrix passes with only the approved deltas: shared request exhaustion, bounded output-size retry, complete-or-omit failed response, aggregate successful-run usage, exact causal restoration, and candidate tracing outside usage.\n- [ ] No bespoke fresh-Agent output-attempt loop remains — output repair is the in-Agent bounded retry (`retries={\"output\":N}`), not a scheduler reconstructing an Agent per attempt. A structured call whose first response is well-formed performs EXACTLY ONE outer model run; the ONLY sanctioned second is the bug-895c native->prompted downgrade. The oracle counts `agent.run_sync` invocations (the outer model runs), NOT `Agent` constructions: exactly one on the happy path, exactly two (native then prompted) on the 895c downgrade, never a third.\n- [ ] `structured_retry_limit` is retained as the output-retry-allowance cap; `structured_retry_limit=0` yields zero output-repair retries (single-shot), preserving the overlap/judge.py and contracts.py batch abstain fail-safe; verified by a dedicated test.\n- [ ] Existing provider fallback eligibility/order, tool limits, timeout/transport ownership, provenance, final artifact policy, and serialized output shapes remain green in their historical regression suites.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] Direct Anthropic and Bedrock canaries pass with secrets absent from output and artifacts; the ticket records timestamp, model ids, request counts, cache read/write/input counts, complete/omit branch, and successful typed result.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, the focused suite, module-size gate, and complexity ratchet pass with `runner.py` at or below 800 lines and every new module between 100 and 500 lines.\n- [ ] The documented retry-layer and accounting contracts match runtime behavior, including the normal plan-reviewed follow-up to raise the shared allowance if legitimate recovery is starved."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786979306511233001,"uuid":"f04f32af-fb71-4f76-9229-0dd9b5bca8f7"}